**Read:** [Chemistry 2e — Flowers, Theopold, Langley, and Robinson](../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94), chapter 15’s solubility and precipitation material; [Analytical Chemistry 2.1 — David Harvey](../sources/Analytical-Chemistry-Harvey.lingua#r_0XMY75E3FRXRGR3BK28V3JAC0T), chapter 6’s solubility and competing-equilibrium examples; [Chem1 General Chemistry Virtual Textbook — Stephen Lower](../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ), solubility products and important solubility systems.

A solubility equilibrium relates the activities of specified dissolved species to a specified solid phase under conditions. Distinguish the ion activity product Q from K_sp and verify whether the solid phase is present or can form on the observation timescale. Different polymorphs, hydrates, complexes, or reaction products can require different models.

For a hypothetical simple MX solid dissociating to monovalent M⁺ and X⁻ in an ideal concentration model, let K_sp,c = 1.00×10⁻¹⁰ (mol L⁻¹)². In pure water with only that dissolution contribution, s = 1.00×10⁻⁵ mol L⁻¹. With a defined 0.0100 mol L⁻¹ common X⁻ background and no other chemistry, the small-s approximation gives s ≈ 1.00×10⁻⁸ mol L⁻¹. The exact balance can test the approximation.

Analytical total concentration can differ from free-ion concentration because of acid/base change, complex formation, or colloids. Precipitation onset, collected yield, particle behavior, and equilibrium solubility are distinct observations. A visually clear sample alone does not establish the absence of a thermodynamic supersaturation.

**Work:** derive the teaching predictions, solve the exact common-ion equation, and check total/charge balances. Add a ligand or pH-dependent anion reaction and identify why the simple solubility estimate changes. Determine which additional constants and observations would be needed rather than inserting an unexplained correction.

Read the full solubility/equilibrium chapter and laboratory activity. Later minerals, water treatment, refining, and analytical separations use these shared concepts with species-specific evidence and process/transport knowledge.

